Sung Moon Kang wrote:

> Let me explain....
>
> We just got a new DEC Personal Workstation 433au.  And because I
> didn't have all the correct information with me when I first
> started up the machine with its factory installed Digital UNIX 4.0D,
> I gave the machine a name... let's just for the sake of argument say
> that I called it BADNAME because I couldn't think of anything else.
>
> Now later, to bring the machine in line with the entire school's
> ethernet name servers and such, it was changed to GOODNAME.  And
> everything is just fine.
>
> However, during one my random trips thro the filesystem (probably
> while installing something), I noticed that there were a few
> files and directories in /usr/ that had the old BADNAME.  From the
> various documentation, I think these all refer to the name of the
> kernel.
>
> Now since I'm probably not going to be taking care of this machine
> into perpetuity, I'd like to change these file names to GOODNAME so
> that it doesn't confuse the next poor guy.  However, I'm concerned
> that changing the directory names and file names from BADNAME.* to
> GOODNAME.* isn't enough... that when I next reboot the machine, it
> won't be able to find the kernel to load to make the machine run.
>
> Could anyone tell me what other things need to be changed from BADNAME
> to GOODNAME to ensure that the machine will reboot okay?  Or do I
> have to compile yet another kernel, altho I'm not making any hardware
> changes?
>
> Please email.  Many thanks in advance.
>
> ---------------------------------------------------------------------
> Sung Moon Kang BK94 AA96     | UCLA Mech and Aero Engineering
> sung.kang.bk.94@aya.yale.edu | http://home.att.net/~sungmoonkang/

cleanest way :

Just go to /sys/conf/

# cp BADNAME GOODNAME
edit GODDNAME and change 1st line (ident) to good.

# doconfig -c GOODNAME
# mv /sys/GOODNAME/vmunix /

add goodname in /etc/hosts with your ip Address
then edit /etc/rc.config and change hostname

Tha's all you'll have to do


