We have a number of X terminals that logs up against a number of
servers. The server are all on the same subnet (130.225.73) and all X
terminals are on the same subnet (130.225.84). In between the two
subnets there is one router.

In order for the X terminals to boot properly, we have an older server
on the same subnet as the X terminals, that forwards them to one of the
servers on the 130.225.73 subnet for their software. This takes place
using traditional bootp.

As the forwarding server is a quite old machine, we would like to be
able to boot the X terminals directly through the router to the
130.225.73-server. Bootp does not seem to work (it hears no messages).
Replacing bootp with joind (in inittab of the server) at least the X
terminal broadcasts are heard, leaving stamps in the syslog:

Sep  2 09:58:30 wessel joind[977]: unregistered host
<MAC>=<00:60:b0:4a:26:f0> sending DHCPDISCOVER -- ignored

So the broadcasts does get though. Can anyone give some help on how to
configure joind to this network topology?

