According to my newsreader, CCURLEY@worldnet.att.net posted...
// I have become the proud owner of several
// HP Apollo 9000 715/75 systems.
// 
// I am waiting for my copy of "UNIX FOR DUMMIES" to arrive, but could use some
// help getting past the LOGIN screen.
// 
// I don't have any way to locate the original users, systems have been in
// storage for some time.
// 
// What I would like to find out, is if there is some magic spell that I could
// type in at the command line (if that's what it's called) to by pass the
// login so I can use the system software on the hard driv.
// 
// As I mentioned before, I am new to UNIX: so please give the direction so
// that THIS DUMMY can use then.

Try 'thompson' at the login prompt.  :)

A Windows-lovin' friend of mine, who was also waiting for his UNIX FOR 
DUMMIES book to arrive, called me up one day:

him:	"How do you change a user's password?"
me:	"Whattaya got?"
him:	"I'm logged in, and have a pound-sign for a prompt."
me:	"Type 'passwd' and the user's login, then <ENTER>."
him:	"OK...wait a minute...OK!  Great!  Got another question."
me:	"Shoot."
him:	"How do you install Kerberos?"

(This is a true story.)

My point is that you're asking a question which is answerable on the 
one hand (i.e., it's probable that someone knowledgable would be able 
to obtain access to your machines) but opens up a whole can of 
complexity when the necessary knowledge just isn't there.

My $0.02: Find a local Unix consultant and pay them to get you 
started, or even call HP if you've got (or had) a service contract 
with them.  My strong guess is there's nothing in UNIX FOR DUMMIES 
that'll help you past this first hurdle.

-- 
