What needs to happen is you need to interrupt the ISL(Initial System Loader)
and boot into
single user mode, if possible. There is a switch that will require root passwd
if set, usually it is not.
If it is set, only the root passwd will allow you to get by this point.

At the "CM>" prompt, type "hp-ux -is" and this will boot you into single user
mode.

Mount partitions if need be. (/var, /usr, /tmp, etc.)

If you are able to gain access and change the root passwd, exercice caution
because as root on
a UNIX (HP-UX) system, you have the power.

Type in "uname -a" and get the version number. If they have been sitting around
awhile, you may still have version 9.X and that OS is not going to be Y2K
compliant. You will have to upgrade to
10.X or 11.0 and still install patches to be truely up to date.

By the way,  the "UNIX for DUMMIES" book will talk very little (if at all)
about the Logical
Volume Manager (LVM). If the HPs are using LVM, get a truely HP-UX book and
study.

